<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Capitals of the World</h1><p class="ws-lede">Eight cities where power lived.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/capitals-of-the-world">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Latin</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Silk Road</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Babylon</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Gate of the gods: the ancient world's byword for the great city.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Chang'an</b>: &#8220;The Tang capital, the largest city on earth and the eastern end of the Silk Road.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 8, <b>Tenochtitlan</b>: &#8220;The island capital on the lake, larger than the European cities of its conquerors.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Eight cities where power lived.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/capitals-of-the-world. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Capitals of the World</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Latin</dt><dd>Latin is the language of ancient Rome, which spread across the western Mediterranean with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages.</dd><dt>Silk Road</dt><dd>The Silk Road was the network of overland trade routes linking China with Central Asia, Iran, the Middle East and the Mediterranean.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
